  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-2941",
      "knowledge_point_id": "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem",
      "problem": "A rocket that is launched vertically is tracked by a radar station located on\nthe ground $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the rocket at the instant when its distance from the radar station is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $1\\,000\\,\\text{km/h}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069977/assets/7af033f137fd76e5e3003926a598e695f4e0852b11dc27dfa84d58040144972b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch point and the rocket, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $z=5\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =16 \\\\ y & =4\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1\\,000\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch point ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)(0)+2(4)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(1\\,000) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{10\\,000}{8} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =1\\,250\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the rocket is $1\\,250\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$",
      "answer_fields": [
