  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070089",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-122092",
      "knowledge_point_id": "e9a12b49-8f77-5480-a3c7-c291e9549af1",
      "knowledge_point": "Calculating the Velocity Vector at a Point in Time Given the Acceleration Vector",
      "problem": "The acceleration $a$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\na(t)=⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle has velocity $v=⟨-1,1⟩$ when $t=2,$ calculate the velocity vector of $P$ at time $t=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the velocity vector, we need to integrate the acceleration vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\int a(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨1-3{t}^{2},4t-1⟩\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int (1-3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t,\\int (4t-1)\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+{C}_{1},2{t}^{2}-t+{C}_{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $v(2)=⟨-1,1⟩.$ Substituting $t=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(2) & =⟨2-{2}^{3}+{C}_{1},2({2}^{2})-2+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨-1,1⟩ & =⟨-6+{C}_{1},6+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have ${C}_{1}=5$ and ${C}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, the velocity is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =⟨t-{t}^{3}+5,2{t}^{2}-t-5⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the velocity at time $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(1) & =⟨1-{1}^{3}+5,2({1}^{2})-1-5⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,-4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
