  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070204",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-116504",
      "knowledge_point_id": "d0703e1b-2b81-5b95-b2ad-a7e42e702c21",
      "knowledge_point": "Applying the Second Fundamental Theorem of Calculus Using the Chain Rule",
      "problem": "Determine ${f}^{′}(x)$ if $f(x)={\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute ${f}^{′}(x),$ we first make the substitution $u=3x-5$ and then we apply the chain rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{3x-5}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit $u$ is located on the lower limit. To apply FTC II, we need the variable to be on the upper limit. Using the properties of the definite integrals, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{u}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}(-{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by FTC II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}u}{\\int }_{1}^{u}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t-1)\\,\\text{d}t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3x-5)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2u-1)) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(3x-5)-1) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x-11).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
