      "math_academy_id": "q-131541",
      "knowledge_point_id": "98bd56e8-77ca-553a-9c26-04feae0618dd",
      "knowledge_point": "Computing the Probability of a Normal Random Variable on an Unbounded Interval",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(0,{10}^{2}),$ compute $P(X<-11.7).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.1251\\,$ | $\\,0.1230\\,$ | $\\,0.1210\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1469\\,$ | $\\,0.1446\\,$ | $\\,0.1423\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1711\\,$ | $\\,0.1685\\,$ | $\\,0.1660\\,$ |",
      "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $X∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X<x)=P(Z<\\frac{x-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =10.$\n\nFirst, we transform $X$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-11.7) & =P(Z<\\frac{-11.7-0}{10}) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.17) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le -1.17) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.17)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070250/assets/9b47010de67e748c75465b0ad1ec54d984ec7eaac6721ae9fb9b11cb60c16b16.png)\n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(-1.17)=0.1210.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(X<-11.7)=0.1210.\n$$",
