  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14070321",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-132316",
      "knowledge_point_id": "45c41284-7cb9-5581-9b8f-5bbfce5aad0c",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Missing Parameter Using Symmetry When the Mean Is Nonzero",
      "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $Φ(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2})$ is a normal random variable with $P(-5\\mu <X<7\\mu )=0.5762,$ determine $\\mu .$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.7580\\,$ | $\\,0.7611\\,$ | $\\,0.7642\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.7881\\,$ | $\\,0.7910\\,$ | $\\,0.7939\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.8159\\,$ | $\\,0.8186\\,$ | $\\,0.8212\\,$ |\n\n*Hint: Note that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$*",
      "worked_solution": "Note that we can write the given probability as\n\n$$\nP(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu )=0.5762,\n$$\n\nwhich tells us that the interval $-5\\mu <X<7\\mu $ is symmetric about the mean $\\mu .$\n\nFirst, we find the cumulative probability using symmetry:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\mu -6\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.5762 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-P(X<\\mu ) & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu )-0.5 & =0.2881 \\\\ P(X<\\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le \\mu +6\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we transform to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 7\\mu ) & =0.7881 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{7\\mu -\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{6\\mu }{15}) & =0.7881 \\\\ \\frac{6\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881) \\\\ \\frac{2\\mu }{5} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nAccording to the table, we have\n\n$$\nΦ(0.8)=0.7881\\,⇒\\,{Φ}^{-1}(0.7881)=0.8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\mu }{5} & =0.8 \\\\ 2\\mu  & =4 \\\\ \\mu  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
